  "id": "COMPOUND:15180 - PROTEIN:5854",
  "verbalization": "Acronyculatin E is implicated in the pathogenesis of LNCaP, a prostate cancer cell line, through a mechanism involving CDK3/cyclin E. This relationship is supported by multiple evidence paths, all of which point to CDK3/cyclin E as a common target. The drug Acronyculatin E is shown to interact with CDK3/cyclin E, which is a key regulator of the cell cycle and has been identified as a target of several compounds, including 2-Hexenyl-beta-glucopyranoside, (+)-Phomenone, and GlyTouCan:G99562EF. These compounds, in turn, target LNCaP, suggesting a potential role for Acronyculatin E in modulating the activity of CDK3/cyclin E and thereby influencing the proliferation of LNCaP cells. The consistent targeting of CDK3/cyclin E across different compounds and cell lines supports a mechanistic link between Acronyculatin E and LNCaP, though the exact mode of action remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence suggests that Acronyculatin E may act through a pathway that involves CDK3/cyclin E, potentially offering a novel therapeutic approach for prostate cancer.",
